Description: Save the state of a session's toplevels when the session is destroyed.
 Destroying a session preserves the current state of its toplevels, as the protocol describes, but mutter saves
 the state of a toplevel only when its window is unmanaged, which is skipped once the session is gone. When a
 client quits without destroying its windows first, libwayland destroys its objects in the order of their IDs, so
 a window whose surface, toplevel and xdg_wm_base all have higher IDs than the session is unmanaged only after the
 session, and mutter forgets where it was. GTK 4 binds xdg_wm_base and creates its later windows after its
 session, and quits without destroying its windows, so Text Editor, quitting with several windows open, had only
 its first window placed again at the next start. Mutter now saves the state of every toplevel still having a
 window when the session is destroyed, unless the client removed the session. A toplevel's surface is looked
 up only while it is set, as on_window_unmanaging clears it when the window goes away.
 .
 The test xdg-session-management-restore is run a second time with the client destroying its session while its
 toplevel is still mapped (restore-session-destroyed), which fails without the change.
Author: Mitar
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-04
